From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 21:33:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] bdj: search the built-in locations when LIBBLURAY_CP has no
 jar of this version

The Java half of libbluray (libbluray-j2se-<version>.jar and its AWT
companion) is found under the name of this very version only, because
the two halves call each other by signature: since 1.5.0 the native side
builds org.videolan.TIMark objects with the constructor (IIJ)V, while
the 1.4.0 classes have only (IIJJJI)V. A jar of another version cannot
work, and the version in the file name is what keeps it out.

LIBBLURAY_CP overrides the search, and when the jar is not where it
points to, _find_libbluray_jar0() gave up ("invalid LIBBLURAY_CP")
without looking anywhere else. LibreELEC's "JRE for BD-J menus" add-on
(tools.jre.zulu) sets LIBBLURAY_CP to its own folder in a profile that
every Kodi start reads, and it ships the jar of the libbluray it was
built with. Its build in the repository for LibreELEC 13
(tools.jre.zulu-12.80.7.0, 2026-05-07) carries libbluray-j2se-1.4.0.jar,
so with libbluray 1.5.0 or 1.5.1 BD-J menus never run, even where a jar
of the right version is installed next to the library: the add-on's
variable hides it.

When LIBBLURAY_CP does not hold a readable jar, say so and search the
built-in locations, as without it: the folder of the library, its
../share/java, /usr/share/java and /usr/share/libbluray/lib. Only a jar
with this version's name is found there, so a jar of another version is
still never used. A LIBBLURAY_CP that does hold a readable jar is used
as before; when it names a file of another name, that is said in the
log, because such a jar can fail.

The result of the BD-J check is now in the log at the level of the other
errors (DBG_CRIT): which JVM and which jar were used, or, when no JVM
could be loaded, which JAVA_HOME was tried. Kodi passes DBG_CRIT to its
log, so a disc whose menus do not run says why without a debug log.
---
 src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c | 36 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c b/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c
index 543ec11cdbfc..005aad8189a7 100644
--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c
@@ -571,15 +571,36 @@ static char *_find_libbluray_jar0()
         }
 
         if (_can_read_file(jar)) {
+            const char *base = jar, *p;
+            for (p = jar; *p; p++) {
+                if (*p == '/' || *p == '\\') {
+                    base = p + 1;
+                }
+            }
+            if (strcmp(base, BDJ_JARFILE)) {
+                BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ | DBG_CRIT, "LIBBLURAY_CP names %s, not " BDJ_JARFILE
+                         ": a jar of another libbluray version can fail\n", jar);
+            }
             return jar;
         }
 
         X_FREE(jar);
         BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ | DBG_CRIT, "invalid LIBBLURAY_CP %s\n", classpath);
-        return NULL;
-    }
 
-    BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ, "LIBBLURAY_CP not set, searching for "BDJ_JARFILE" ...\n");
+        /*
+         * The jar and this library are two halves of one program: the native side builds
+         * objects of the jar's classes through constructors named by their signature (1.5.0
+         * changed the one of org.videolan.TIMark), so only the jar of this very version can
+         * work with it, and that is why its name carries the version. A LIBBLURAY_CP that
+         * does not hold that jar (an add-on that ships the jar of the libbluray it was built
+         * with) is no reason to give up the copy installed with this library: search the
+         * built-in locations, as without LIBBLURAY_CP.
+         */
+        BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ | DBG_CRIT, "LIBBLURAY_CP has no " BDJ_JARFILE
+                 ", searching the built-in locations\n");
+    } else {
+        BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ, "LIBBLURAY_CP not set, searching for "BDJ_JARFILE" ...\n");
+    }
 
     // check directory where libbluray.so was loaded from
     const char *lib_path = dl_get_path();
@@ -792,7 +813,11 @@ int bdj_jvm_available(BDJ_CONFIG *storage)
     const char *java_home = NULL;
     void* jvm_lib = _load_jvm(&java_home, storage->java_home);
     if (!jvm_lib) {
-        BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ | DBG_CRIT, "BD-J check: Failed to load JVM library\n");
+        const char *env_home = getenv("JAVA_HOME");
+        BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ | DBG_CRIT, "BD-J check: Failed to load JVM library (%s%s)\n",
+                 storage->java_home ? "JAVA_HOME of the application: " :
+                 env_home ? "JAVA_HOME: " : "JAVA_HOME not set, built-in locations",
+                 storage->java_home ? storage->java_home : env_home ? env_home : "");
         return BDJ_CHECK_NO_JVM;
     }
     dl_dlclose(jvm_lib);
@@ -802,7 +827,8 @@ int bdj_jvm_available(BDJ_CONFIG *storage)
         return BDJ_CHECK_NO_JAR;
     }
 
-    BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ, "BD-J check: OK\n");
+    BD_DEBUG(DBG_BDJ | DBG_CRIT, "BD-J check: OK, JVM from %s, %s\n",
+             java_home ? java_home : "?", storage->classpath[0]);
 
     return BDJ_CHECK_OK;
 }
